**Annotation.** A form of metadata attached to code (classes, methods, fields, parameters) using `@Name`. It does not change logic by itself; the compiler, tools or frameworks read it. Examples: `@Override` (compiler checks you really override), `@Deprecated`, `@SuppressWarnings("unchecked")`, `@FunctionalInterface`, and framework ones such as `@Autowired` or `@Test`. Custom annotations are declared with `@interface`, with meta-annotations like `@Retention` and `@Target`.

**Functional interface.** An interface with exactly **one abstract method** (default and static methods are allowed, and so are methods inherited from `Object`). It is the target type for lambda expressions and method references. `@FunctionalInterface` is optional but makes the compiler reject a second abstract method. Built-ins: `Runnable`, `Comparator`, `Function`, `Predicate`, `Consumer`, `Supplier`.

**Write and use one.**

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface Calculator {
    int apply(int a, int b);
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calculator add = (a, b) -> a + b;
        Calculator mul = (a, b) -> a * b;
        System.out.println(add.apply(3, 4)); // 7
        System.out.println(mul.apply(3, 4)); // 12
    }
}
```

The lambda supplies the body of `apply`. You could also pass a `Calculator` to a method (`static int run(Calculator c, int x, int y)`), which is the behaviour-as-parameter idea.

**Link to polymorphism.** The same variable type `Calculator` holds different behaviours, resolved at run time. A lambda is a concise alternative to an anonymous class.

**Common slips in a live editor:** forgetting that a lambda needs a functional interface target, putting two abstract methods in the interface, missing `static` on helper methods called from `main`.
